<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lower Lusatia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Niederlausitz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a> is a historical region in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cottbus">Cottbus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spree">Spree</a> Forest, home to the Lower Sorbian Slavic minority. Ruled as a margraviate under Wettin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> and Bohemian lords, it passed with the Bohemian crown and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> in 1635 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1815. It is distinguished from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-lusatia">Upper Lusatia</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bautzen">Bautzen</a> to the south.</p></div>
